      "section": "hero.case-header",
      "content": {
        "eyebrow": "(CASE STUDY · 02)",
        "title": "FIGCOMS",
        "disciplines": [
          "Product Design",
          "UX | UI",
          "Art Direction",
          "Motion & 3D"
        ],
        "summary": "Figcoms is a workflow platform built on a simple idea: Figma comments are powerful, but the work around them is broken. Instead of pulling teams out of Figma, Figcoms extends it - turning scattered comments into a structured, syncable workflow that lives where the work already happens.",
        "leadMedia": {
          "assetRole": "client-case-media",
          "alt": "Figcoms - the review board, walkthrough",
          "src": "/projects/figcoms/ui-design-1.jpg"
        },
        "meta": [
          {
            "label": "ROLE",
            "value": "Brand & Product Design"
          },
          {
            "label": "TIMELINE",
            "value": "4 Months"
          },
          {
            "label": "YEAR",
            "value": "2025"
          },
          {
            "label": "TEAM",
            "value": "Co-Founder"
          }
        ]
      },
      "motion": {
        "pattern": "page-enter",
        "reducedMotionFallback": "static-final-state"
      }
    },
    {
      "section": "work.case-role",
      "content": {
        "eyebrow": "(MY ROLE)",
        "points": [
          "Co-founded Figcoms and designed the product: the board, the four states, and the flow between them.",
          "Replaced the first control set with an emoji-based status system once it started adding complexity instead of removing it.",
          "Built the identity, the motion language and the landing page in parallel with the app."
        ]
      },
      "motion": {
        "pattern": "reveal-rise",
        "reducedMotionFallback": "static-final-state"
      }
    },
    {
      "section": "work.case-context",
      "content": {
        "items": [
          {
            "label": "(WHY IT EXISTS)",
            "body": "Out of work with the design and dev teams at Elementor and Salesforce. Everyone comments inside Figma; past ten frames nobody can tell what is waiting, working, in review or done."
          },
          {
            "label": "(THE FIRST VERSION DID TOO MUCH)",
            "body": "The early controls turned every comment into a form. They were cut for four states and an emoji."
          },
          {
            "label": "(AFTER LAUNCH)",
            "body": "Two people, four months. #33 on Product Hunt the day it shipped, and used on real projects since."
          }
        ]
      },
      "motion": {
        "pattern": "reveal-rise",
        "reducedMotionFallback": "static-final-state"
      }
    },
    {
      "section": "work.case-process",
      "content": {
        "title": "Process",
        "steps": [
          {
            "index": "01",
            "title": "Research & Context",
            "body": "We mapped how teams actually use Figma comments today. The pain pointed to ownership, priority, and continuity - not to Figma itself."
          },
          {
            "index": "02",
            "title": "Concept & Flow",
            "body": "The core mechanic became “one link, instant clarity.” Paste a Figma URL, get an organized workspace built around four states every team already speaks."
          },
          {
            "index": "03",
            "title": "UX/UI & Interaction",
            "body": "Soft cards, generous whitespace, and a typographic hierarchy that lets the comment lead. Emoji-based status changes replaced heavy controls."
          },
          {
            "index": "04",
            "title": "System & Experience",
            "body": "Identity, motion language, and landing page were built in parallel with the app so marketing and product speak one sentence."
          }
        ]
      },
      "motion": {
        "pattern": "reveal-rise",
        "reducedMotionFallback": "static-final-state"
      }
    },
